CLOSURE_CHECKS_CLASSIFIER_AUDIT: {
  "schema_version": 1,
  "migration_run_id": "274893c2-7895-4fb2-8644-16e05d91c71c",
  "snapshot_timestamp": "2026-05-21T18:16:44Z",
  "ticket_id": "1414-362e",
  "items": [
    {
      "original_text": "During brainstorm, after approach selection (post-Phase 2 Step 1), a cross-epic interaction scan loads the Approach and Success Criteria sections from all open/in-progress epics and compares them against the new epic's proposed approach using haiku-tier agent dispatch. When no open epics exist (N=0), the scan completes with an empty interaction set and logs \"No open epics \u2014 scan skipped.\" When the haiku agent dispatch fails, the scan degrades gracefully: logs a warning and continues without interaction signals. When the number of open epics exceeds 20, the scan batches them into groups of 20 and dispatches parallel haiku sub-agents (one per batch), merging results before classification \u2014 all epics are compared regardless of total count.",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "end-state",
        "ranking": 4,
        "rationale": "describes durable system behavior and capabilities for cross-epic interaction scanning that will persist once implemented"
      },
      "user_decision": "auto",
      "post_migration_section": "SC"
    },
    {
      "original_text": "Each detected interaction is classified into one of four severity categories: benign (shared namespace but non-overlapping \u2014 logged, no action), consideration (integration detail needed \u2014 propagated as acceptance criteria), ambiguity (overlapping claim on same resource requiring human decision \u2014 halts for user resolution), conflict (mutually exclusive strategies for same resource \u2014 forces approach revision).",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "end-state",
        "ranking": 4,
        "rationale": "describes durable system behavior for how interactions are classified and handled, not a one-time migration or completion task"
      },
      "user_decision": "auto",
      "post_migration_section": "SC"
    },
    {
      "original_text": "Each injected consideration-level acceptance criterion references three required fields: (a) the specific shared resource (e.g., route path /api/users, config key auth.provider, DB table sessions), (b) the overlapping epic ID and title, and (c) a falsifiable integration constraint (e.g., \"GET /api/users must return 200 whether auth-epic middleware is active or not\"). Criteria lacking any of these three fields are rejected by the classifier. Before injection, duplicate signals referencing the same shared resource across multiple overlapping epics are deduplicated to a single AC. These structured ACs propagate through preplanning to implementation-plan to task execution as verifiable done definitions.",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "end-state",
        "ranking": 4,
        "rationale": "describes a durable system property where acceptance criteria are structured with three required fields and deduplicated across epics, persisting as verifiable definitions throughout planning and execution"
      },
      "user_decision": "auto",
      "post_migration_section": "SC"
    },
    {
      "original_text": "Ambiguity and conflict classifications halt the brainstorm pipeline and present the interaction to the practitioner with both epics' relevant context, the specific resource overlap, and why the interaction is problematic. On ambiguity: the practitioner selects from (a) designate one epic as namespace owner, or (b) accept shared usage with an explicit integration contract written as AC. On conflict: the practitioner chooses one approach as authoritative; the other epic is flagged for approach revision. After resolution, the scan re-runs against the revised spec, bounded by brainstorm.max_interaction_cycles (default: 2). In non-interactive mode, ambiguity and conflict classifications are recorded as INTERACTIVITY_DEFERRED ticket comments and the epic is tagged interaction:deferred. Preplanning and implementation-plan entry gates check for this tag and block decomposition until the finding is resolved interactively, at which point the tag is removed.",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "end-state",
        "ranking": 4,
        "rationale": "describes durable system behavior where ambiguities and conflicts halt the pipeline and present context to practitioners, with defined resolution paths and gate checks"
      },
      "user_decision": "auto",
      "post_migration_section": "SC"
    },
    {
      "original_text": "When a consideration-level interaction involves two epics both registering user-navigable handlers or pages under the same URL prefix (e.g., both claim /<slug>/ patterns or both define routes under /admin/*), the injected AC includes a cross-feature Playwright navigation assertion that exercises at least one URL in the overlap zone and asserts the expected HTTP status and content from each epic's perspective in a combined routing context. Non-navigable overlaps (API-only endpoints, webhook receivers, middleware-internal paths) receive standard integration ACs without Playwright assertions.",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "end-state",
        "ranking": 4,
        "rationale": "describes a durable system behavior for how the AC injection process handles overlapping URL patterns, phrased as ongoing capability"
      },
      "user_decision": "auto",
      "post_migration_section": "SC"
    },
    {
      "original_text": "A fixture test provides two synthetic epic stubs with a known URL namespace collision (both claim /api/users) and one benign shared dependency (both use PostgreSQL with non-overlapping schemas). Running the interaction classifier against this fixture produces: (a) a conflict-tier or ambiguity-tier signal naming /api/users as the overlapping resource, (b) a benign-tier signal for the shared DB dependency, and (c) no false positives. This fixture test is runnable and green before the epic closes.",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "end-state",
        "ranking": 4,
        "rationale": "describes a durable testing capability that validates the classifier's behavior against known collision scenarios"
      },
      "user_decision": "auto",
      "post_migration_section": "SC"
    }
  ]
}
